  <h2>What people mean by black mold</h2>
  <p>Most of the time, "black mold" means <em>Stachybotrys chartarum</em>. It needs material that stays wet for a long time, such as drywall paper, ceiling tile or wood after a slow leak or flood, which is why it's a sign of a serious moisture problem. But other common molds, including species of <em>Cladosporium</em>, <em>Alternaria</em> and <em>Aspergillus</em>, can also look black or dark green, and they're far more common on bathroom grout, window sills and damp basements.</p>
  <p>The species matters less than the moisture. Mold won't grow without water, and it will come back after cleaning if the leak, condensation or humidity isn't fixed.</p>

  <h2>Health effects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Most people:</strong> mold can cause stuffy nose, sore throat, coughing or wheezing, burning eyes and skin rash.</li>
    <li><strong>People with allergies or asthma:</strong> reactions can be stronger, and mold can trigger asthma attacks.</li>
    <li><strong>People with weakened immune systems or chronic lung disease:</strong> are at higher risk of infection and should avoid moldy areas and cleanup work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you have symptoms you think are related to mold, see a doctor. Mold remediation companies can remove mold, but they can't diagnose health problems.</p>

  <h2>Can you clean it yourself?</h2>
  <p>The EPA's guidance for homeowners uses a size threshold of about 10 square feet. Below that, cleanup is usually a do-it-yourself job if the mold isn't from sewage and no one in the house is at higher risk.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Fix the water first.</strong> Repair the leak, dry the area, and lower humidity below 60% (ideally 30% to 50%).</li>
    <li><strong>Protect yourself.</strong> Wear an N95 respirator, gloves and goggles without vent holes.</li>
    <li><strong>Scrub hard surfaces</strong> with detergent and water, then dry them completely.</li>
    <li><strong>Remove porous materials</strong> that are moldy, such as drywall, ceiling tile, insulation and carpet. Bag them before carrying them through the house.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't paint or caulk over mold.</strong> It keeps growing underneath and the paint will peel.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>When to call a remediation company</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The growth covers more than about 10 square feet, or keeps coming back.</li>
    <li>It's inside walls, under floors, in the attic or crawl space, or in the heating and cooling system.</li>
    <li>It followed a sewage backup or floodwater.</li>
    <li>Someone in the house has asthma, a weakened immune system, or symptoms you think are mold-related.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Professional remediation under the IICRC S520 standard sets up containment with plastic sheeting and negative air pressure so spores don't spread, removes contaminated materials, cleans with HEPA vacuums, and dries the area. Ask the company whether it also fixes the moisture source or whether you'll need a separate contractor.</p>

  <h2>When mold testing makes sense</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You smell mold but can't find it, and want to narrow down where it is.</li>
    <li>You want clearance testing after a professional remediation, done by an independent assessor rather than the company that did the work.</li>
    <li>A landlord, buyer, insurer or court requires documentation.</li>
  </ul>
